The Security Council,

Recalling and reaffirming all its previous resolutions on Western Sahara,

Expressing its full support for the Secretary-General and his Personal Envoy, Staffan de Mistura, in their efforts to advance the political process and implement relevant Security Council resolutions, including through sustained consultations with Morocco, the Frente POLISARIO, Algeria, and Mauritania,

Reaffirming its commitment to assist the parties to achieve a just, lasting, and mutually acceptable political solution based on compromise, consistent with the principles and purposes of the Charter of the United Nations, and noting the role and responsibilities of the parties in this respect,

Recognizing the continued momentum and the urgent need for the parties to reach a definitive resolution to this long-standing dispute, and acknowledging the essential role played by the United Nations Mission for the Referendum in Western Sahara (MINURSO) in creating a conducive environment for political negotiations and supporting the Personal Envoy,

Taking note of the growing international support expressed for Morocco’s Autonomy Proposal, presented on 11 April 2007 to the Secretary-General, as a serious, credible, and realistic basis for a just, lasting, and mutually acceptable solution, and reaffirming that genuine autonomy under Moroccan sovereignty represents the most feasible outcome to satisfy the aspirations of the people of Western Sahara,

Welcoming the ongoing diplomatic efforts convened by the Personal Envoy to bring the parties together in direct and substantive negotiations, and reiterating appreciation for international initiatives and venues offered to facilitate an enduring political settlement,

Underlining the vital importance of the full adherence to the ceasefire, calling upon all parties to refrain from any actions that could destabilize the region or jeopardize the political process, and urging full cooperation with MINURSO, including unhindered freedom of movement and safe, regular resupply of its team sites,

Noting with deep concern the living conditions and underfunding of assistance for Sahrawi refugees in Tindouf, urging the international community to provide increased humanitarian assistance, and reiterating its request for the registration of refugees in the Tindouf refugee camps,

Having considered the report of the Secretary-General of October 2026,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of MINURSO until 31 October 2027;

2. Expresses its full support for the Secretary-General and his Personal Envoy in conducting negotiations taking Morocco’s Autonomy Proposal as the basis to achieve a final, just, and lasting resolution to the dispute, consistent with the principles of the Charter of the United Nations;

3. Calls upon Morocco, the Frente POLISARIO, Algeria, and Mauritania to engage in these negotiations in good faith and without preconditions, taking into account the efforts made and developments since 2006, with a view to achieving a final and mutually acceptable political solution that provides for self-determination through an agreed autonomy framework;

4. Welcomes the findings of the strategic review of MINURSO submitted by the Secretary-General and requests the Secretary-General to initiate transition planning and assessment for the Mission, aligning MINURSO’s operational posture and footprint with progress achieved in the political negotiations;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to brief the Security Council regularly, at least every six months, on the status and progress of negotiations and MINURSO’s operations, and to keep the Council closely apprised of any development affecting regional stability;

6. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

