Noting with deep concern the living conditions and underfunding of assistance for Sahrawi refugees in Tindouf, urging the international community to provide increased humanitarian assistance, and reiterating its request for the registration of refugees in the Tindouf refugee camps,

Having considered the report of the Secretary-General of October 2026,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of MINURSO until 31 October 2027;

2. Expresses its full support for the Secretary-General and his Personal Envoy in conducting negotiations taking Morocco’s Autonomy Proposal as the basis to achieve a final, just, and lasting resolution to the dispute, consistent with the principles and purposes of the Charter of the United Nations, and welcomes constructive suggestions by the parties;

3. Calls upon Morocco, the Frente POLISARIO, Algeria, and Mauritania to engage in these negotiations in good faith and without preconditions, taking into account the efforts made and developments since 2006, with a view to achieving a final and mutually acceptable political solution that provides for the self-determination of the people of Western Sahara in the context of arrangements consistent with the principles and purposes of the Charter of the United Nations, recognizing that genuine autonomy could represent a most feasible outcome;

4. Welcomes the findings of the strategic review of MINURSO submitted by the Secretary-General, underscores the importance of the Mission's operational effectiveness and safety, and requests the Secretary-General to continue assessing the Mission's operational requirements and future footprint, in alignment with progress achieved in the political negotiations;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to brief the Security Council regularly, at least every six months, on the status and progress of negotiations and MINURSO’s operations, and to keep the Council closely apprised of any development affecting regional stability;

6. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

